# The ONUM Variable Axis

Written by Jake Maddox
=> releases.tsv Version 1.00

## Introduction

The ONUM variable axis is used for interpolation between lining and old-style figures, allowing for users to choose any height they desire for old-style figures. This level of control was previously only available to the type designer.

This variable axis is designed to replace the 𝚘𝚗𝚞𝚖 and 𝚕𝚗𝚞𝚖 OpenType features.

## Conventions

* ONUM=0, the minimum value, represents lining figures.
* ONUM=1, the maximum value, represents old-style figures at x-height.

## CSS

Traditionally, this CSS was used to choose between lining and old-style figures:

```code
font-variant-numeric: lining-nums;
font-variant-numeric: oldstyle-nums;
```

Using the ONUM axis, the code becomes

```code
font-variation-settings: "ONUM" 0; /* lining figures */
font-variation-settings: "ONUM" 1; /* x-height old-style figures */
```

The advantage of the ONUM axis is that it is also possible to go between 0 and 1. An example:

```code
font-variation-settings: "ONUM" 0.5;
```
